  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1043 PM CDT TUE AUG 11 2026\n\nCentral Alabama will increasingly come under the influence of\nridging building in across the southern United States through the\nrest of the week and beyond. Wednesday will feature another\nchance of showers and storms across eastern Central Alabama, but\ncoverage at this time looks to be less than today. As the ridge\nbuilds in late week, those chances for rain become less,\nespecially across western parts of the region, where no rain is\nin the forecast through at least Monday. Latest ECMWF, GFS, and\nother global models bring a demoralizing 598 to 600 decameter\nridge and center it over the lower Mississippi Valley this\nweekend. The term demoralizing is used as a ridge near 600 dm\nwill mean high temperatures pushing higher than we've seen this\nyear. As Saturday rolls around and becomes Sunday and Monday, we\nare currently forecast to see high temperatures pushing or\nexceeding 100\u00b0F across a broad part of the area, especially along\nand west of the I-65 corridor. Even more concerning is that the \ninfluence of the ridge looks to last through next week, even \nbeyond the current forecast period. With less ground moisture due \nto an overall lack of precipitation moving forward, high \ntemperatures may be allowed to exceed 100\u00b0F going into the 7-9 \nday range from now. This is especially concerning for heat stress \non people and animals, as well as increasing concerns with \nvegetation and the return of drought conditions heading toward the\nend of next week.\n\nIn the meantime, a Heat Advisory will go into effect at 1 pm\nWednesday afternoon for most of Central Alabama, minus the\neastern and southeastern counties. Heat index values will begin\ncreeping above the 105\u00b0F mark. This will most likely be expanded \narea-wide for Thursday at the next forecast update, and will \nlikely be carried through the weekend. Nightly low temperatures \nwill provide little relief from the heat, staying in the mid to \nupper 70s in most places south and west in Central Alabama. Higher\nterrain of northeast Alabama will stay a few degrees cooler \nthrough this whole run, with lows in the low 70s and highs in the \nmid 90s.\n\n12",
